{"question_id": "caf03d32", "hypothesis": "A few slow-cooker habits make a big difference\u2014especially for stews and the plant-based dishes you\u2019ve been exploring:\n\n- **Check the fill level:** Aim for about **\u00bd to \u2154 full**. Too little can cook unevenly; too full can leave the center undercooked.\n- **Use less liquid than you would on the stove:** The lid traps moisture, so ingredients release plenty of liquid. As a starting point, use roughly **\u2154\u2013\u00be of the recipe\u2019s stovetop liquid**, unless it\u2019s a soup.\n- **Cut ingredients evenly, and put dense vegetables low:** Potatoes, carrots, and other firm vegetables go near the bottom; more delicate vegetables go on top or in during the last hour or so.\n- **Keep the lid on:** Each peek lets heat escape and can add around **15\u201320 minutes** to cooking time. Check near the end, not repeatedly.\n- **Brown meat and aromatics when you can:** It\u2019s optional, but browning beef or onions first adds flavor\u2014useful for the beef stew you\u2019ve made.\n- **Add quick-cooking ingredients late:** Pasta, leafy greens, dairy, and fresh herbs can turn mushy or lose flavor if cooked all day. Stir them in near the end.\n- **Taste and adjust at the end:** Slow cooking can mute seasoning. Add salt, pepper, acid (lemon or vinegar), or fresh herbs after cooking; thicken a watery stew with a little starch slurry.\n\nIf you tell me what\u2019s going wrong\u2014too watery, bland, overcooked, or something else\u2014and which recipe you tried, I can help pinpoint the fix.", "autoeval_label": {"model": "openai/gpt-4o-2024-08-06", "label": false}}
